Apple is moving ahead with changes that could make it easier to use separate work and personal numbers in third-party iPhone calling and contact apps, after advancing a request to the development stage this week.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/iphone-dual-sim.jpg" alt="" width="1600" height="900" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1077985" /><br/>
The <a href="https://developer.apple.com/eu-interoperability-request/fb24237463/">request</a> was submitted by developer Airelec Clima on August 9 and would let these apps use more of the information iOS already keeps about a user's two cellular lines.<br/>
<br/>
Today, third-party apps lack a supported way to read the names users give their cellular lines, such as "Work" and "Personal," or read and change the preferred line saved for each contact in iOS. The proposed changes would make that information available to eligible apps.<br/>
<br/>
For example, someone could use a third-party contact app to choose their work number for calling a colleague. Apple's Contacts and Phone apps would then share that preference. The third-party app could also display the user's familiar "Work" and "Personal" labels, making it easier to tell the two numbers apart.<br/>
<br/>
The developer also wants apps to be able to tell which line was used for a call in call history, where that information is available. This could let an alternative calling app show whether an incoming call came through the user's work or personal number.<br/>
<br/>
The request does not seek access to sensitive SIM identifiers or subscription credentials. The developer says apps would only need the line's label and an identifier that tells one cellular service apart from another.<br/>
<br/>
The request was made through Apple's <a href="https://developer.apple.com/support/ios-interoperability/">EU interoperability process</a>, which lets developers ask for access to iPhone and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a> features used by Apple's own apps or hardware. Apple operates the process to help meet its obligations under the EU's Digital Markets Act (DMA).<br/>
<br/>
The law <a href="https://digital-markets-act.ec.europa.eu/citizens-and-whistleblower-portal/eu-citizens-qa_en">requires Apple to give third parties access</a> to the same operating system features available to its own services and devices, so competing products can work as seamlessly with the platform. Although this request falls under the EU process, the resulting changes could be made available more widely.<br/>
<br/>
